<p class="article__lede">A multimodal model can describe a photo, read a chart, or answer a question about a video frame. It can also make you wait. Most of that wait isn't mysterious: it comes from a few places you can measure, and each one has well-known ways to win time back.</p>

<h4>1. Measure before you optimize</h4>
<p>Split a request into stages and time each one:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Preprocessing:</strong> decoding, resizing and normalizing the image or video frames.</li>
  <li><strong>Vision encoding:</strong> turning pixels into visual tokens.</li>
  <li><strong>Prefill:</strong> the language model reading the prompt plus every visual token, once.</li>
  <li><strong>Decode:</strong> generating the answer one token at a time.</li>
</ul>
<p>Two numbers matter to users: time to first token (mostly preprocessing, encoding and prefill) and tokens per second after that (mostly decode). They respond to different fixes, so know which one is hurting before you change anything.</p>

<h4>2. Spend fewer tokens on pixels</h4>
<p>Images are expensive because they become a lot of tokens. LLaVA-1.5, for example, turns a single 336×336 image into 576 visual tokens before the model has read a word of your question, and high-resolution or multi-image inputs multiply that. Every extra token costs compute during prefill and memory in the KV cache for the rest of the conversation.</p>
<p>The cheapest speedup is often just sending less: resize to what the task needs, crop to the region that matters, sample fewer frames from video, and, where the model supports it, merge or prune redundant visual tokens. Check accuracy after each change; some tasks, like reading small text, genuinely need the pixels.</p>

<h4>3. Don't recompute what you already know</h4>
<p>If someone asks three questions about the same image, the image only needs to be encoded once. Cache image embeddings by content hash, reuse the KV cache for shared prefixes (a long system prompt, or the same image across turns), and keep models loaded and warm instead of paying start-up costs per request.</p>

<h4>4. Make every operation cheaper, then verify</h4>
<p>Quantization is the workhorse. Moving weights (and, where accuracy allows, activations) from 32-bit floats to INT8 makes a model about 4× smaller and cuts memory traffic accordingly; 4-bit weight formats go further for large language models. When I quantized an ECG anomaly detector to INT8 to run as Verilog on a Zynq-7020 FPGA, the 4× reduction was the difference between fitting on the chip and not.</p>
<p>The rule I hold myself to: optimize, then verify. When I exported a behavior-recognition engine to ONNX, I checked that the exported model agreed with the original on 100% of predictions before trusting the speedup. Faster and wrong is not faster.</p>
<p>Beyond quantization, fused attention kernels (FlashAttention-style) cut memory reads and writes, and runtimes such as ONNX Runtime and TensorRT fuse operators and pick efficient kernels for your hardware.</p>

<h4>5. Decode faster</h4>
<p>Generating tokens one at a time is usually limited by memory bandwidth rather than arithmetic. Speculative decoding helps: a small draft model proposes several tokens and the large model checks them in a single pass, without changing what the large model would have produced. When serving many users, continuous batching and paged KV-cache management (the approach popularized by vLLM) keep the accelerator busy instead of idle between requests. Streaming tokens as they're generated doesn't make the model faster, but it makes the wait feel much shorter.</p>

<h4>6. Don't call the big model when a small one will do</h4>
<p>The fastest inference is the one you skip. Put cheap models or rules in front and save the expensive model for the inputs that need it. The same idea shaped the behavior engine I built at ApexFlo, which runs at 18.6 FPS on a laptop CPU with no GPU: one shared skeleton backbone, a learned model (an ST-GCN) only where it's needed for fall detection, and simple rules for person-down and loitering.</p>

<h4>7. Overlap the stages</h4>
<p>Preprocessing often runs on the CPU while the accelerator waits. Pipeline it: decode and resize the next frame while the current one is being encoded, batch small images together, and copy data to the accelerator asynchronously. For video, process frames as a stream rather than collecting a whole clip first.</p>

<h4>A short checklist</h4>
<ol>
  <li>Time each stage; know whether time to first token or tokens per second is the problem.</li>
  <li>Send fewer visual tokens: resize, crop, sample, merge.</li>
  <li>Cache image embeddings and shared prefixes.</li>
  <li>Quantize, fuse and use a good runtime, then verify accuracy.</li>
  <li>Speed up decoding with speculation, batching and streaming.</li>
  <li>Route easy inputs to small models.</li>
  <li>Overlap preprocessing with compute.</li>
</ol>
<p>None of this is exotic. The skill is in measuring honestly, choosing the fixes that match where your time actually goes, and checking that the answers are still right.</p>
